            <h1>Van defect report template UK: free download + how to record defects.</h1>
            <p class="page-lede">A defect found during a walkaround is not a compliance failure. A defect with no record is. This page covers the legal obligations around van defect recording, what a defect report must include, and what happens when the follow-up evidence is missing.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-tight">
        <div class="long-form-body">
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>Finding a defect is not the problem. Having no record of it, or no evidence it was acted on, is.</li>
                    <li>Defect records must include who found it, when, what it was, and what was done about it.</li>
                    <li>A defect report without a follow-up repair record proves you knew about the problem.</li>
                    <li>DVSA can request defect records at any time. They must be producible quickly.</li>
                    <li>Digital records with timestamps are more defensible than paper forms in a loose folder.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h2>The legal position on van defect records</h2>
            <p>Under the Road Traffic Act 1988, using a motor vehicle in a dangerous condition on a public road is an offence. The obligation sits with both driver and operator. The driver must not move a vehicle they know to be unroadworthy. The operator must not permit it.</p>
            <p>DVSA enforcement guidance requires operators to have a documented system for defect reporting. A culture of verbal reporting, engineers telling the depot manager by phone, is not a system. It leaves no evidence that the defect was known, assessed, or fixed.</p>
            <p>The Traffic Commissioners look at defect management when assessing operator compliance. A pattern of defects with no follow-up action is treated as evidence of systemic management failure, not a clerical problem.</p>
            <p>For van operators without a formal operator's licence, the same legal framework applies. The Road Traffic Act does not distinguish between a fleet of 3 vans and a fleet of 300.</p>
            <p>If you knew about the defect and the vehicle caused an incident, the defect record proves you knew. The absence of a repair record proves you did nothing. That is the worst position to be in.</p>

            <h2>What a defect report must include</h2>
            <p>There is no single prescribed format. DVSA and the Traffic Commissioners have published guidance on what they expect, and the consistent elements are as follows.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Vehicle registration</strong>: which vehicle the defect was found on.</li>
                <li><strong>Date and time</strong>: when the defect was discovered.</li>
                <li><strong>Driver's name</strong>: who found and reported it.</li>
                <li><strong>Description of the defect</strong>: specific enough to be unambiguous. "Tyre issue" is not sufficient. "Near-side rear tyre: tread depth 2mm, small cut in sidewall" is sufficient.</li>
                <li><strong>Location on the vehicle</strong>: which corner, which panel, which component.</li>
                <li><strong>Severity assessment</strong>: is the vehicle safe to use? If the answer is no, it must be taken out of service.</li>
                <li><strong>Photograph</strong>: not mandatory but strongly recommended. A photograph removes ambiguity about severity.</li>
                <li><strong>Action taken</strong>: repair completed, vehicle stood down, defect monitored. This must be recorded separately and linked back to the original defect report.</li>
                <li><strong>Sign-off</strong>: who authorised the vehicle to return to service and when.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout">
                The photograph is the element most often skipped. An engineer who photographs the cracked wing mirror before driving off has evidence that it was minor cosmetic damage. An engineer who does not has only a written description, and written descriptions are always less persuasive than images.
            </div>

